      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the first two terms of the arithmetic sequence given that the fifth term is 11 and the common difference is 1.\n\n1. **Identify the general form of an arithmetic sequence:**\n   The \\(n\\)-th term of an arithmetic sequence can be expressed as:\n   \\[\n   a_n = a_1 + (n-1)d\n   \\]\n   where \\(a_n\\) is the \\(n\\)-th term, \\(a_1\\) is the first term, \\(d\\) is the common difference, and \\(n\\) is the term number.\n\n2. **Use the given information to set up an equation:**\n   We know that the fifth term \\(a_5 = 11\\) and the common difference \\(d = 1\\). Substituting these values into the general form, we get:\n   \\[\n   a_5 = a_1 + (5-1) \\cdot 1 = a_1 + 4 \\cdot 1 = a_1 + 4\n   \\]\n   Since \\(a_5 = 11\\), we can write:\n   \\[\n   a_1 + 4 = 11\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\(a_1\\), we get:\n   \\[\n   a_1 = 11 - 4 = 7\n   \\]\n\n3. **Find the first two terms:**\n   The first term \\(a_1\\) is 7, and the second term \\(a_2\\) can be found using the formula for the second term:\n   \\[\n   a_2 = a_1 + d = 7 + 1 = 8\n   \\]\n   So, the first two terms of the sequence are 7 and 8.\n\n4. **Calculate the product of the first two terms:**\n   The product of the first two terms is:\n   \\[\n   a_1 \\cdot a_2 = 7 \\cdot 8 = 56\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the product of the first two terms is \\(\\boxed{56}\\).",
